From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] v4l2_codec2: encode HEVC Main 10 from P010 input

The HEVC encoder offered Main only. EncodeInterface dropped Main 10
because the encoder always configured NV12 input, so no app could get
a hardware 10-bit HEVC stream (the software HEVC encoder has no Main 10
either). A device that takes 10-bit input can now code Main 10:

- Capabilities: a device may list Main 10 in its profile menu without
  offering a 10-bit input format. queryEncodingCapabilities() now sets
  HEVC as the coded format on a probe instance (the raw formats of a
  stateful encoder may depend on it) and looks for P010 among the raw
  formats. Main 10 is kept only when the device lists P010 and gralloc
  can allocate it; otherwise the component offers Main as before.
- Pixel format: with Main 10 the interface declares
  C2StreamPixelFormatInfo::input {IMPLEMENTATION_DEFINED, YCBCR_420_888,
  YCBCR_P010}, as the software AV1 encoder does, so the framework lists
  COLOR_FormatYUVP010. Its setter takes any value: the component reads
  the layout of every input block anyway, and configure() must not start
  failing for a color format that worked before (e.g. RGBA byte buffers).
- Input format: a Main 10 session configures single-planar P010 (V4L2
  has no multi-planar P010) and takes the stride from a P010 block. It
  no longer falls back to the device's preferred formats, which are
  8-bit: a Main 10 stream coded from NV12 would lose the precision of
  its input, and the firmware reads the input of a Main 10 session as
  10-bit samples whatever its format.
- Detection: getVideoFrameLayout() and FormatConverter classified a P010
  block as NV12 (semi-planar, U first), so a P010 frame given to the
  encoder was coded as an NV12 picture with the wrong stride. They now
  tell P010 apart by its 10-bit samples in 16-bit words.
- Device layout: P010 is single-planar, so the device reads a frame
  from the start of the buffer at its own stride (bytesperline), with
  the chroma plane at that stride times the format height; there is no
  way to pass a frame's own stride or chroma offset. A P010 frame in
  that layout is queued as is. Every other frame goes through the format
  convertor, which writes its P010 frames in the device's layout rather
  than in the layout gralloc gives its blocks (the same with minigbm, not
  guaranteed elsewhere), after checking that the frames fit in each
  block.
- Other inputs: an 8-bit YUV frame in a Main 10 session is widened to
  P010 instead of being refused. A client may pair any listed color
  format with any listed profile, widening is exact (an 8-bit code
  becomes the 10-bit code four times its value, in limited and full
  range alike), and only that combination pays for the pass over the
  frame. An RGB frame (an input surface rendered with GL, a screen
  capture) is converted as in an 8-bit session, with the BT.601 matrix
  into limited range at 8 bits, and then widened: apps that find Main 10
  listed may use it with any input they used with Main, and an 8-bit
  RGB frame has no more precision to keep. A P010 frame in an 8-bit
  session (a 10-bit decoder rendering into an encoder's input surface)
  is rounded to NV12 instead of being coded as garbage.
- P010 helpers: bytesPerElement() counted two bytes for an element of
  the UV plane, which holds a U and a V sample, so allocationSize() and
  the bytesused of a queued P010 frame came out a sixth short.
  videoPixelFormatToString(P010) returned "BGRA". The V4L2 frame layout
  of single-planar P010 is now derived like that of NV12.

8-bit sessions are unchanged: their input format, stride query, format
conversions and buffers are as before, and the AVC encoder and HEVC on
a device without P010 input declare the same parameters as before.

